---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`How the ${site.year} ${site.course} portfolio of ${site.author} is organised, sourced, and written.`}
>
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow page-head" slot="banner">
    <p class="eyebrow">About this portfolio</p>
    <h1 class="page-head__title">How this site works</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow entry-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        This is my {site.year} portfolio for {site.course} at {site.school}. It
        collects the official portfolio entries we were set during the {site.schoolYear.label}
        school year, and it replaces the Class Notebook we used in Year 1.
      </p>

      <h2>How the entries are organised</h2>
      <p>
        Every entry has a title, a date, and a number. The numbering is
        chronological, so entry 01 is the first piece of work of the school year
        and the newest entry always sits at the top of the home page. The row of
        markers under the masthead shows the whole school year at a glance: a
        filled dot is a finished entry, and a dashed outline is a month that is
        still empty. It is there so that anyone reading this — including me — can
        see straight away whether the portfolio is complete.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each entry ends with the same two blocks: <strong>Sources</strong> and
        <strong>How I used AI</strong>. They are part of the page template rather
        than something I remember to add, which means no entry can be published
        without them.
      </p>

      <h2>How I use AI in this portfolio</h2>
      <p>
        I use AI tools, and I would rather be precise about it than vague. My rule
        is simple: <strong>the thinking and the reflection are mine, and the words
        are mine.</strong> AI is useful to me in three narrow ways.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Checking my English.</strong> I am not a native speaker, so I
          sometimes ask a model to point out grammar mistakes or unnatural phrasing
          in a paragraph I have already written. I decide which corrections to take.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Explaining something I did not understand.</strong> If a technical
          term from class is unclear, I ask for an explanation and then verify it
          against a second, citable source before I write about it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Building this website.</strong> The code behind the site was
          written with the help of an AI coding assistant. That is a tool choice,
          not a shortcut around the writing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        What I do not do is ask a model to write an entry for me. A reflection that
        I did not think through would be worth nothing to me, and it would be
        obvious to read. Where AI made a real difference to a specific entry, the
        "How I used AI" block on that entry says exactly what it did.
      </p>

      <h2>Colophon</h2>
      <p>
        The site is built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> and deployed
        on Vercel. Each entry is a Markdown file kept in a Git repository, so the
        full history of what I wrote and when is preserved. Type is set in Archivo
        for headings and interface text, Newsreader for reading, and JetBrains Mono
        for dates and labels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Written and maintained by {site.author} for {site.teacher}.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
